---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="CritPages | Chris Dabel"
  description="A critique app for unpublished writing, built solo with Claude Code. The architecture decisions that kept four hundred AI-written commits from becoming a Frankenstein app."
>
  <h1>CritPages</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The critique app for unpublished writing. Founder and sole engineer. Built
    with Claude Code, with me as the architect in the loop, in beta since
    mid 2026.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="why">
    <h2 id="why">Why I built it</h2>
    <p>
      So that writers and literature buffs anywhere in the world can share
      their writing freely and give each other real critique and feedback.
      Beta reads, peer story sharing, non-fiction too.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="what">
    <h2 id="what">What it is</h2>
    <p>
      Writers share pages with a critique group, a beta-reader round, or a
      single partner. Readers mark them up on their phones: a tag, a strike, a
      heart, or a comment attached to a word, a sentence, or a paragraph, each
      in about two taps. Each reader sees only their own marks. The writer sees
      all of them together. Groups can read live, everyone following along on
      their own device while one person reads aloud.
    </p>
    <p>
      Native apps for iPhone and Android. The web handles invitations,
      accounts, finding beta readers, and scheduling. Manuscripts never touch
      the web.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="stack">
    <h2 id="stack">Stack</h2>
    <ul class="stack">
      <li>Expo and React Native, TypeScript, with the manuscript painted by Skia rather than rendered as text</li>
      <li>Supabase: Postgres with row-level security, realtime, and edge functions</li>
      <li>Next.js on Vercel Pro for the web, plain and portable by rule</li>
      <li>Resend for every email the software sends</li>
      <li>Route 53 DNS as Terraform, email records included</li>
      <li>Maestro end-to-end flows on real devices, and an RLS suite run against the live database</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="decisions">
    <h2 id="decisions">What kept it from becoming a Frankenstein app</h2>
    <p>
      Four hundred commits in fifteen weeks, most of the code written by
      Claude Code with me directing. The model is not what kept it coherent. A
      short list of ranked rules did, plus the habit of recording every
      decision, and its reasoning, in the commit that made it. These are the
      ones that mattered.
    </p>
    <dl class="decisions">
      <div>
        <dt>Ranked goals, written down before the first screen</dt>
        <dd>
          The conventions file ranks four goals and says the higher one wins
          when they conflict: correctness and testability, then privacy and
          trust, then cheap change, then maintainability for a solo author who
          comes back after months away. A counterweight sits under them: ship
          the MVP. Every "helpful" addition the model proposed was tested
          against that list, and most lost.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The security boundary lives in Postgres, and is tested against the live database</dt>
        <dd>
          The first migration was the schema and its row-level security
          policies together. The next day an integration suite ran those
          policies against the real database, and caught a real gap on its
          first run. That suite has grown to about 150 assertions covering
          document scoping, per-reviewer isolation, frozen-text immutability,
          and lifecycle. Unpublished manuscripts are the data; a scoping mistake
          is a privacy incident with the users least likely to forgive it.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Supabase is called in exactly one layer</dt>
        <dd>
          Domain code is pure and knows nothing about React or the database.
          Services orchestrate it through repository interfaces. The repository
          layer is the only place the Supabase client appears, and the UI calls
          services, never repositories. Dependencies point inward. The same
          file forbids speculative abstraction: no interface until there is a
          second implementation or a test seam that needs it.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Spikes go behind a flag, and the loser gets deleted</dt>
        <dd>
          The reader started as ordinary React Native text. A Skia canvas
          renderer was built as a spike behind a renderer boundary and a flag,
          retired every risk that could kill the switch, and ten days later
          became the only renderer. The legacy path, both flags, the swap plan,
          and thirteen hundred lines went with it. Drawing the page instead of
          rendering text is also why a manuscript cannot be copied out.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>A decision stands until its premise dies</dt>
        <dd>
          A spike opened all of Moby Dick as one document on the simulator and
          settled the question: no chapter splitting for MVP. The same day a
          Galaxy S24 Ultra killed the app at 316,000 words. The decision was
          reversed in a commit that names the dead premise, chooses chapter
          splitting at import because it is additive code rather than surgery
          on the most invariant-dense file in the project, and parks the
          windowed reader with its two known failure modes recorded.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Decision gates that say no</dt>
        <dd>
          A document-to-workspace many-to-many was designed, then deferred at
          the gate: the survival benefit already shipped through soft delete,
          and the only unique payoff did not clearly beat a copy. What shipped
          instead was a re-architecture onto a link model, author-owned pages
          shared by links, with sessions that end themselves.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Renames go full depth, and migrations are history</dt>
        <dd>
          When "group" became "workspace" and "facilitator" became "owner",
          the rename went through the schema, not just the UI. An applied
          migration is never edited, even when its header carries the
          product's old name. The newest migration that touches a table is the
          present; the rest are dated records of what was true that day.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The code is canonical, and the cuts are deliberate</dt>
        <dd>
          The spec that started the project was demoted to rationale once the
          code outgrew it. What still binds from it is the list of things
          absent on purpose: no page cap, no save buttons, no AI in the
          product, no drag-to-select. The conventions file tells the model not
          to reintroduce them as helpful additions.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The web is plain on purpose</dt>
        <dd>
          Full Next.js rather than a static export, because static to
          server-rendered is a migration and the reverse is a config change.
          No vendor-only APIs, so the host can move in an afternoon. Netlify was
          tried and rejected the same day, with the reasons written down.
          Marketing at the apex, the product on a subdomain, DNS in Terraform.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Built for the readers it actually has</dt>
        <dd>
          Writing circles include retirees with declining vision. The app was
          walked on an iPhone SE at the largest accessibility text size, every
          broken control was measured and capped inside the shared components,
          and the painted manuscript now follows the phone's text setting until
          the reader picks their own.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="status">
    <h2 id="status">Status</h2>
    <p>
      Pre-launch beta. Early access is open by email at
      <a href="https://critpages.com">critpages.com</a>. Product notes go out
      on <a href="https://critpages.substack.com">the CritPages Substack</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

